# The script `mise run ffi-test` has the editor's headless mode run against
# the config in `config/`, under the editor's input scheme and with the
# library's fuzzy matcher, to ask for a prompt with candidates, to choose
# one with the keys the editor ships and to accept it. What it prints must
# be exactly `expected`.

reload
settle

# `pick` is config's: it asks `Pick: ` with the completion source config
# registers under `picks`, by name, whose candidates are `wq`, `edit` and
# `w`. A `w` is typed into the minibuffer. The minibuffer's status line is
# the prompt's label alone, and the scene lists the candidates the text
# matches in the candidate list, a view of its own after the minibuffer's,
# one a line, each in the candidate face with the `w` it matched in the
# match face: `w` and then `wq`. The library's matcher scores the two the same,
# and `wq` came first, so only the rule that a candidate that is the text
# is first puts `w` before it. `edit` holds no `w` and is not listed.
run pick
key w KeyW -
scene

# `<Tab>` moves to the next candidate, with the compiled command
# `minibuffer-next`: under the Vim scheme the minibuffer's view is in a Vim
# state of its own, which binds the key. Once chooses `w`, twice `wq`,
# whose row of the list is then in the face of the chosen candidate. The
# minibuffer's text is still `w`.
key <Tab> Tab -
key <Tab> Tab -
scene

# `<Enter>` accepts, and config's `picked` receives the chosen candidate,
# not the typed text.
key <Enter> Enter -
scene

# A text no candidate matches: the list is empty, though its view stays in
# the scene, since the list's place grows and never shrinks while the
# prompt is open (the empty initial text listed all three). It took its
# view id the first time it was drawn, so this prompt's minibuffer is view
# 3. `<Tab>` chooses nothing, and `<Enter>` gives `picked` the typed text.
run pick
key z KeyZ -
scene
key <Tab> Tab -
key <Enter> Enter -
scene
